WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:22.269
We're in a period right now that
gender scholars have called

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:23.687
the Stalled Gender Revolution.

00:00:23.687 --> 00:00:27.622
And what this means,
if you look at all kinds of data,

00:00:27.622 --> 00:00:32.433
what you see is in the 1970s we
started to see a rapid closing,

00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:35.250
and narrowing of gender inequality.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.020
So the gender wage gap, for example,
starts to go down rapidly in the 1970s.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.720
More women are flooding in the paid labor,
women are being promoted.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.400
You get to the 1980s,
progress is still being made.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.078
Although it's a little bit slower,
and by the time we hit the mid 90s,

00:00:50.078 --> 00:00:51.780
change has kind of flattened out.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.500
So things aren't getting worse, but

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.700
we haven't seen a lot of progress in
the move towards gender equality lately.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.770
So we're a bit stuck and

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.700
my take on that, the reason that we're
stuck is that we haven't effectively

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.430
changed work enough to accommodate
who's actually in our workplaces today.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.083
And then,

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:13.664
once we do that we're going to see a jump
start towards more gender equality

00:01:13.664 --> 00:01:18.159
[MUSIC]

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.310
One thing that we know is stereotypes
do limit women's advancement.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:26.750
But they do so not because people
necessarily have explicit stereotypes

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.120
about men versus women, but
that they have more unconscious or

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.860
implicit kind of associations about what
men are like and what women are like.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.420
And those implicit associations then
affect how we see someone's performance,

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.380
how we judge their behavior and
can creates sort of slight differences and

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.820
the ways that we've been evaluate men and
women in the workplace.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.040
So this unconscious and explicit advice is
I think are one of the biggest barriers

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.420
that we're still up against today,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.030
when we think about how we can
move more women into leadership.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:58.040
Often we have two narrow
of a conception of leaders.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.884
Leaders are these autocratic kind of
to risk taking sort of people, and our

00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:06.640
stereotypes of that leaders about leaders
overlap with our stereotypes about man.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.180
Such that men often look naturally
more leader like to us, but

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.890
if we broad in this stereotypes of
leadership, that creates a more expansive

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.540
view of leadership and one in which women
are likely to be seen as more successful.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.205
So if stereotypes are causing us to see
some people as more talented than they

00:02:23.205 --> 00:02:26.873
are and other people as less talented
than they are, it's interfering with

00:02:26.873 --> 00:02:30.106
the goal of being truly meritocratic and
is blocking innovation.

00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:34.505
[MUSIC]

00:02:34.505 --> 00:02:38.281
In what ways can we introduce policies,
and procedures, and

00:02:38.281 --> 00:02:42.492
tools into the workplace that cause
people, once stereotypes creep

00:02:42.492 --> 00:02:47.450
into their head, to be aware of that, and
stop and move beyond the stereotypes?

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.050
So the goal is rather than relying
on some stereotype about how men and

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.500
women are, we would instead have
some sort of procedure in place,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.340
that would allow us to more effectively
evaluate the true talents and

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.890
abilities of people and therefore block
the effects of bias among judgments.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.850
We have to help people first see biases.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:11.980
So I do a lot of work with teaching people
about the way that stereotypes work.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.610
Because until you see how stereotypes
work, you're not going to be aware of how

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.990
it could be affecting your judgement, and
I should just add, just to be clear here

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:23.400
is that the stereotypes affect all of
our judgments, women as well as men.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.110
We see that very clearly.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:29.180
You've rarely find that a situation where
men are lot more biased than women.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.120
These are unconscious stereotypes.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.011
We have to help people see them first so
that seeing bias is kind of key.

00:03:35.011 --> 00:03:39.546
[MUSIC]

00:03:39.546 --> 00:03:42.345
One of things that has been really
encouraging to me in this work is how many

00:03:42.345 --> 00:03:44.390
really well intentioned
people there are out there.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.860
Men and women who want to get beyond bias.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.240
So the question is can
we help them do that?

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.060
So what I've been doing is working with

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.680
a lot of different companies
to help them create tools.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.620
And so, we have some ideas about
the kinds of things that work in general.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.400
So for example, we know that when
people have clear criteria for

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.200
say a promotion decision,
that's very clearly articulated.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:12.640
And I read and study that criteria
before they evaluate job applicants,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.120
stereotypes are much less likely
to affect their judgments.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.630
One thing that we noticed that
when people are making decisions

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:22.580
is sometimes the criteria they use
to judge people will shift around

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.680
depending upon whether they're
evaluating a woman or a man.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.640
I saw a great example that comes from
psychology, shows that when people

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.240
were evaluating psychology faculty they
were way more likely to say, well,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.377
I would need to see if she was a good
teacher before I could hire her.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:41.035
But that criteria hadn't been raised
when they were evaluating a man so

00:04:41.035 --> 00:04:42.925
it's a perfectly legitimate criteria.

00:04:42.925 --> 00:04:45.339
You would want a professor
to be a good teacher, but

00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:48.157
it was being applied much more
heavily to women than to men.

00:04:48.157 --> 00:04:49.621
So one of our tools is for

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:54.287
someone in the group to be noticing
when new criteria are being raised.

00:04:54.287 --> 00:04:57.157
And to say something like,
if we're going to consider teaching I

00:04:57.157 --> 00:04:59.957
think we should go back and
consider that for all of the candidates.

00:04:59.957 --> 00:05:01.427
So that we're using the same criteria.

00:05:01.427 --> 00:05:04.800
That too blocks the use of stereotypes.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.060
So there's a variety of kinds of things
like that, that we can do to not eliminate

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.445
the unconscious stereotypes that
are happening in our heads but

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.325
to block their negative effects and

00:05:14.325 --> 00:05:18.294
to be sure that we're evaluating people
by criteria and not by stereotypes

00:05:18.294 --> 00:05:23.009
[MUSIC]

00:05:23.009 --> 00:05:26.459
Imagine a company wants to say
do better on its diversity and

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.564
inclusion indicators and
the CEO comes out and says,

00:05:29.564 --> 00:05:34.200
this is one of our main priorities, and
we're really want to work hard on this.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.280
And that's great, they had that kind of
support from the toughest essential, but

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.660
what we find is that support from
the top kind of trickles down.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.890
And it's affecting sort of managers
at kind of higher levels, but

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:49.230
once it gets to kind of the mid level of
the organization, it often gets stuck.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.840
So we got this CEO supporting
change at the top,

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.660
you've got people at the bottom
that are agitating for change, but

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.840
you get to that middle level, and
that's what we called the frozen middle.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.298
It's a place where organizational change
often gets stuck specially in diversity

00:06:02.298 --> 00:06:03.199
and inclusion.

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:06.565
And so,
our goal in working on these projects,

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:09.830
is to really try work
at that frozen middle.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.510
That is to develop these
tools to block bias

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:15.200
by working directly with people managers.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.726
And by working directly with people
managers, what we are hoping to do

00:06:18.726 --> 00:06:22.552
is enlisting down and the support of
creating the tools helps create buyer,

00:06:22.552 --> 00:06:25.267
at that level we're changed,
otherwise get stuck.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:29.747
[MUSIC]

00:06:29.747 --> 00:06:33.153
There's something that's actually
really quite easy to do and

00:06:33.153 --> 00:06:37.880
that is we can share the accomplishments
of other women in our networks at work.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.460
One thing that we know is that stereotypes
cause women to be seen as less confident

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.770
than they actually are.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.752
And that's what limits
women's advancement.

00:06:45.752 --> 00:06:48.120
Women can't fix this problem themselves.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.660
You can't as a woman, if people
aren't noticing your accomplishments,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.340
you can't just say,
I'm brilliant, for example.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:57.000
There'd be all kinds of
backlash directed at you.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.650
But one thing that we can do is
taut other women's accomplishments.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:05.510
So I think being very mindful of giving
attention the accomplishments of women in

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.620
the organization is hugely important.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.180
So individuals can do this.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.850
Organizations as well, I think.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.808
Announcing through department
newsletters and things like that,

00:07:15.808 --> 00:07:19.652
the accomplishments of their women
employees are crucially important for

00:07:19.652 --> 00:07:23.801
raising those women's status and for
starting to fracture the stereotypes that

00:07:23.801 --> 00:07:28.031
we have that are leading us to cause us to
see women as less competent then they are.

00:07:28.031 --> 00:07:32.509
[MUSIC]

